NAME
    mv - move or rename files

SYNOPSIS
    mv src dst
    mv glob dir

DESCRIPTION
    Moves or renames src:
        mv OLD.TXT NEW.TXT   the same directory: the entry is renamed
                             in place, nothing is copied
        mv A.TXT /T          into an existing directory: /T/A.TXT
        mv A.TXT /T/B.TXT    another directory: the file is copied
                             (load and exec kept) and src deleted
        mv DIR NEWNAME       a directory is renamed in place; it cannot
                             move to another directory
    A glob source (* or ? in the last component) moves every matching
    file into dst, which must be an existing directory. A file of the
    target name is replaced. mv X X is refused ("mv: same file: X"): it
    would delete the only copy. mv is /BIN/MV.

    Messages: "mv: not found: X", "mv: cannot rename X", "mv: cannot
    replace X" (the new name is a directory), "mv: cannot copy to X",
    "mv: a directory moves only within its directory: X",
    "mv: the target of a glob must be a directory", "mv: no match: X".

LIMITS
    A glob moves at most 24 files. src and dst are cut at 63
    characters. The sectors of a file moved to another directory are
    not reclaimed (no PACK yet).

EXAMPLES
    mv /F.TXT /G.TXT          rename
    mv /G.TXT /T              move into /T
    mv /T/*.TXT /             every .TXT file of /T back to /
    mv /T /TMP                rename a directory

NOTES
    The shell's built-in ren renames too, but never replaces a file.
    Changed from the P8X version: a move within one directory renames
    the entry (P8XFS on the P8X had no rename, so mv always copied and
    deleted); load and exec are kept; directories can be renamed; a
    single file can go into a directory.
    Ported from P8X os/commands/mv.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    ren, cp, del, apath
